data: {"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"intake":{"question":"Should I open a second location for my bakery, or invest in delivery first?","leaning":"Open the second location. A storefront just came up for lease on Route 1.","profile":{"name":"Rise & Shine Bakery","industry":"neighborhood bakery and cafe","location":"College Park, Maryland","size":"1 location, 7 employees","current_tools":["Square POS","Instagram","Google Business Profile"],"budget":"$90,000 in savings","goals":["grow revenue","don't put the first store at risk"],"constraints":"Revenue is about $42,000 a month. Savings cover six months of runway. I bake every morning myself and have no manager yet."}},"evidence_mode":"web"},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"Assemble the shared evidence for this decision: Should I open a second location for my bakery, or invest in delivery first? (neighborhood bakery and cafe, College Park, Maryland)","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/intake@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/intake@1"]},"id":"64373056-59ea-412c-975d-c7a84da9c309","timestamp":1794033241.6860168}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"I'm going to run several independent searches at once, covering the Maryland and Prince George's County licensing, bakery buildout costs, delivery commission rates, minimum wage figures for both the state and county, Route 1 retail lease rates, industry benchmarks, sales tax on food, and delivery tools from Square.\n\nI think I have enough research now. I'm skipping further checks on Maryland sales tax for bakeries and student seasonality in College Park, and using general knowledge for those. The delivery-fee caps, including the Prince George's County question, are unverified, so I'll list them as open questions, along with the rules on personal guarantees in small business leases.\n\nI'm pulling together the concrete figures now: the Prince George's County minimum wage of $15.30, and that College Park sits within that county. I'm also noting Route 1 lease listings at roughly $36 per square foot on average, which works out to about $4,500 a month for 1,500 square feet, and comparing that against a $90k budget and reported bakery startup costs. I'm keeping these as sourced claims rather than doing further calculation.\n\n","thought":true},{"text":"{\"decision\":\"Whether Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) should spend its $90,000 in savings on a second location, such as the Route 1 storefront, or on a delivery channel for the existing store first.\",\"options\":[\"Do nothing for now: keep the single store, build cash and hire or train a manager before any expansion or new channel.\",\"Invest in delivery first: add commission-free own-channel ordering through Square Online with courier dispatch, and optionally test a marketplace such as DoorDash or Uber Eats on a limited basis.\",\"Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and any landlord tenant-improvement allowance or financing.\",\"Hybrid or staged: launch low-cost delivery or pickup ordering and hire a manager now, and revisit a second location, or a smaller or second-generation space, after 6-12 months of data.\"],\"facts\":[{\"claim\":\"Startup-cost estimates for a new bakery or cafe site are large compared with $90,000. One 2026 guide puts a roughly 1,400 sq ft second-generation bakery at about $498,000 total, including about $294,000 build-out and $85,000 equipment. Cafe guides give $150,000-$280,000 for a small second-generation shop.\",\"source\":\"https://wilsonklee.com/guides/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-bakery/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Build-out costs vary widely by space condition. Cafe guides cite $30-$80 per sq ft for second-generation food space and $80-$250 per sq ft for a raw shell. A second-generation space can save $40,000-$80,000. Tenant-improvement allowances for food and beverage space typically run $10-$60 per sq ft.\",\"source\":\"https://tabres.com/articles/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-coffee-shop\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"College Park retail asking rents vary by site. LoopNet lists an average of about $36 per sq ft per year. Specific listings run from about $16-$25 up to $45-$50 per sq ft per year, with typical retail space of 1,000-2,500 sq ft.\",\"source\":\"https://www.loopnet.com/search/retail-space/college-park-md/for-lease/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Prince George's County regulates food service facilities. A new or remodeled facility needs plan review before work starts, with scaled drawings, a menu, a HACCP plan, equipment cut sheets and review fees. It then needs a Health Department Food Service Facility permit and a Use & Occupancy permit from the Department of Permitting, Inspections and Enforcement (DPIE).\",\"source\":\"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/permits/use-occupancy-permit\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Prince George's County requires a County-certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times for restaurant-type operators. Moderate and high priority facilities need one on the premises during all operating hours, plus an approved HACCP plan. A second site would therefore need its own certified manager coverage in addition to the owner's.\",\"source\":\"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/licensing/health-licenses/food-service-facility-fsf-permits\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Maryland's statewide minimum wage is $15.00 in 2026 and the tipped cash wage is $3.63. Prince George's County has a higher local minimum of $15.30 per hour effective January 1, 2026. Wage floors apply to any new hires at either site or for delivery.\",\"source\":\"https://www.employerpass.com/maryland-minimum-wage\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Marketplace delivery commissions are about 15%-30% per delivery order. DoorDash tiers are 15%, 25% and 30%, and Uber Eats tiers are 20%, 25% and 30% after a March 2026 increase. Pickup orders cost about 6%-7%. Several sources say all-in effective cost, with promotions and fees, can reach 30%-40% of the order.\",\"source\":\"https://www.blenderdigital.co/blog/how-much-do-doordash-uber-eats-charge-restaurants\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"The owner's current POS (Square) offers Square Online ordering with no commission: $0 per month on Free, $49 per month on Plus, and $149 per month on Premium. Online card fees are 3.3% + $0.30 on Free and 2.9% + $0.30 on Plus and Premium. Delivery is dispatched through on-demand couriers at about $6.99 per delivery within 5 miles, and the fee can be charged to the customer.\",\"source\":\"https://menuthere.com/en/alternatives/square-online/pricing\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"One comparison estimates that absorbing the courier fee on a $40 own-channel Square delivery costs about $8.45, which is $6.99 plus card fees. Own-channel delivery pays off most clearly when the customer pays the delivery fee and on larger orders.\",\"source\":\"https://paymentreview.com/blog/restaurant-online-ordering-fees/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Retail bakery net profit margins are commonly cited as 4%-9%, with other sources giving 5%-15%. Labor is usually 25%-35% of revenue and food cost 28%-35%. On $42,000 per month, a 4%-9% margin would be roughly $1,700-$3,800 per month before owner pay. These benchmarks are not specific to this bakery.\",\"source\":\"https://www.crestmontcapital.com/blog/bakery-business-financing-statistics-2026\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Bakery-cafe prime costs (COGS plus labor) are cited at 60%-70% of revenue. One source puts the single-location revenue ceiling for a dine-in bakery-cafe at $500K-$900K per year. The owner's current run rate is about $504K per year.\",\"source\":\"https://advisedspaces.com/open/bakery\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Commercial leases for small restaurant tenants typically run 5-10 years and often include personal guarantees and NNN charges (taxes, insurance, common-area costs). A shopping-center listing in College Park shows NNN terms of 5-10 years. Personal guarantees are from general knowledge and not confirmed by search.\",\"source\":\"https://www.commercialsearch.com/commercial-property/us/md/college-park/college-park-shopping-center-1/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A bakery with one owner-baker and no manager faces key-person risk when expanding. Production is typically centralized or duplicated for a second site, and the owner's time is split between two sites. This is general operating knowledge, not from a search result.\",\"source\":\"\",\"verified\":false}],\"open_questions\":[\"What are the Route 1 space's size, asking rent, lease type (NNN or gross), term, personal-guarantee requirement and tenant-improvement allowance?\",\"Is the space a second-generation bakery or restaurant space with a hood, grease trap and restrooms already in place, or does it need a full build-out? What are the contractor quotes and permit timeline?\",\"What is the first store's real monthly profit and owner take-home after paying the owner a market wage? $42,000 in revenue says nothing about margin.\",\"How much of the $90,000 is already needed for working capital or the six-month runway? What would remain after build-out, equipment, deposits and pre-opening losses, and could SBA or other financing supplement it?\",\"Who would manage the first store, or the second, while the owner bakes each morning? What would a hired manager cost, and could the manager hold the Prince George's County Food Service Manager certification?\",\"Can the current kitchen handle added delivery volume, or a second store's production, without new equipment or labor?\",\"How much demand exists for delivery among current customers and nearby residents and students? How seasonal is revenue with the University of Maryland calendar, and would a Route 1 site face the same seasonality or cannibalize the first store?\",\"Do the existing Instagram and Google Business Profile traffic and the current order mix (coffee, pastries, custom orders) point toward delivery-friendly products?\",\"Are there any current Prince George's County or College Park rules on third-party delivery fees, signage or zoning for this Route 1 parcel? The search did not confirm any.\"]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":3766,"promptTokenCount":64126,"thoughtsTokenCount":536,"totalTokenCount":67892},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"researcher","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Whether Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) should spend its $90,000 in savings on a second location, such as the Route 1 storefront, or on a delivery channel for the existing store first.","options":["Do nothing for now: keep the single store, build cash and hire or train a manager before any expansion or new channel.","Invest in delivery first: add commission-free own-channel ordering through Square Online with courier dispatch, and optionally test a marketplace such as DoorDash or Uber Eats on a limited basis.","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and any landlord tenant-improvement allowance or financing.","Hybrid or staged: launch low-cost delivery or pickup ordering and hire a manager now, and revisit a second location, or a smaller or second-generation space, after 6-12 months of data."],"facts":[{"claim":"Startup-cost estimates for a new bakery or cafe site are large compared with $90,000. One 2026 guide puts a roughly 1,400 sq ft second-generation bakery at about $498,000 total, including about $294,000 build-out and $85,000 equipment. Cafe guides give $150,000-$280,000 for a small second-generation shop.","source":"https://wilsonklee.com/guides/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true},{"claim":"Build-out costs vary widely by space condition. Cafe guides cite $30-$80 per sq ft for second-generation food space and $80-$250 per sq ft for a raw shell. A second-generation space can save $40,000-$80,000. Tenant-improvement allowances for food and beverage space typically run $10-$60 per sq ft.","source":"https://tabres.com/articles/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-coffee-shop","verified":true},{"claim":"College Park retail asking rents vary by site. LoopNet lists an average of about $36 per sq ft per year. Specific listings run from about $16-$25 up to $45-$50 per sq ft per year, with typical retail space of 1,000-2,500 sq ft.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/search/retail-space/college-park-md/for-lease/","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County regulates food service facilities. A new or remodeled facility needs plan review before work starts, with scaled drawings, a menu, a HACCP plan, equipment cut sheets and review fees. It then needs a Health Department Food Service Facility permit and a Use & Occupancy permit from the Department of Permitting, Inspections and Enforcement (DPIE).","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/permits/use-occupancy-permit","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a County-certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times for restaurant-type operators. Moderate and high priority facilities need one on the premises during all operating hours, plus an approved HACCP plan. A second site would therefore need its own certified manager coverage in addition to the owner's.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/licensing/health-licenses/food-service-facility-fsf-permits","verified":true},{"claim":"Maryland's statewide minimum wage is $15.00 in 2026 and the tipped cash wage is $3.63. Prince George's County has a higher local minimum of $15.30 per hour effective January 1, 2026. Wage floors apply to any new hires at either site or for delivery.","source":"https://www.employerpass.com/maryland-minimum-wage","verified":true},{"claim":"Marketplace delivery commissions are about 15%-30% per delivery order. DoorDash tiers are 15%, 25% and 30%, and Uber Eats tiers are 20%, 25% and 30% after a March 2026 increase. Pickup orders cost about 6%-7%. Several sources say all-in effective cost, with promotions and fees, can reach 30%-40% of the order.","source":"https://www.blenderdigital.co/blog/how-much-do-doordash-uber-eats-charge-restaurants","verified":true},{"claim":"The owner's current POS (Square) offers Square Online ordering with no commission: $0 per month on Free, $49 per month on Plus, and $149 per month on Premium. Online card fees are 3.3% + $0.30 on Free and 2.9% + $0.30 on Plus and Premium. Delivery is dispatched through on-demand couriers at about $6.99 per delivery within 5 miles, and the fee can be charged to the customer.","source":"https://menuthere.com/en/alternatives/square-online/pricing","verified":true},{"claim":"One comparison estimates that absorbing the courier fee on a $40 own-channel Square delivery costs about $8.45, which is $6.99 plus card fees. Own-channel delivery pays off most clearly when the customer pays the delivery fee and on larger orders.","source":"https://paymentreview.com/blog/restaurant-online-ordering-fees/","verified":true},{"claim":"Retail bakery net profit margins are commonly cited as 4%-9%, with other sources giving 5%-15%. Labor is usually 25%-35% of revenue and food cost 28%-35%. On $42,000 per month, a 4%-9% margin would be roughly $1,700-$3,800 per month before owner pay. These benchmarks are not specific to this bakery.","source":"https://www.crestmontcapital.com/blog/bakery-business-financing-statistics-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"Bakery-cafe prime costs (COGS plus labor) are cited at 60%-70% of revenue. One source puts the single-location revenue ceiling for a dine-in bakery-cafe at $500K-$900K per year. The owner's current run rate is about $504K per year.","source":"https://advisedspaces.com/open/bakery","verified":true},{"claim":"Commercial leases for small restaurant tenants typically run 5-10 years and often include personal guarantees and NNN charges (taxes, insurance, common-area costs). A shopping-center listing in College Park shows NNN terms of 5-10 years. Personal guarantees are from general knowledge and not confirmed by search.","source":"https://www.commercialsearch.com/commercial-property/us/md/college-park/college-park-shopping-center-1/","verified":true},{"claim":"A bakery with one owner-baker and no manager faces key-person risk when expanding. Production is typically centralized or duplicated for a second site, and the owner's time is split between two sites. This is general operating knowledge, not from a search result.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's size, asking rent, lease type (NNN or gross), term, personal-guarantee requirement and tenant-improvement allowance?","Is the space a second-generation bakery or restaurant space with a hood, grease trap and restrooms already in place, or does it need a full build-out? What are the contractor quotes and permit timeline?","What is the first store's real monthly profit and owner take-home after paying the owner a market wage? $42,000 in revenue says nothing about margin.","How much of the $90,000 is already needed for working capital or the six-month runway? What would remain after build-out, equipment, deposits and pre-opening losses, and could SBA or other financing supplement it?","Who would manage the first store, or the second, while the owner bakes each morning? What would a hired manager cost, and could the manager hold the Prince George's County Food Service Manager certification?","Can the current kitchen handle added delivery volume, or a second store's production, without new equipment or labor?","How much demand exists for delivery among current customers and nearby residents and students? How seasonal is revenue with the University of Maryland calendar, and would a Route 1 site face the same seasonality or cannibalize the first store?","Do the existing Instagram and Google Business Profile traffic and the current order mix (coffee, pastries, custom orders) point toward delivery-friendly products?","Are there any current Prince George's County or College Park rules on third-party delivery fees, signage or zoning for this Route 1 parcel? The search did not confirm any."]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Whether Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) should spend its $90,000 in savings on a second location, such as the Route 1 storefront, or on a delivery channel for the existing store first.","options":["Do nothing for now: keep the single store, build cash and hire or train a manager before any expansion or new channel.","Invest in delivery first: add commission-free own-channel ordering through Square Online with courier dispatch, and optionally test a marketplace such as DoorDash or Uber Eats on a limited basis.","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and any landlord tenant-improvement allowance or financing.","Hybrid or staged: launch low-cost delivery or pickup ordering and hire a manager now, and revisit a second location, or a smaller or second-generation space, after 6-12 months of data."],"facts":[{"claim":"Startup-cost estimates for a new bakery or cafe site are large compared with $90,000. One 2026 guide puts a roughly 1,400 sq ft second-generation bakery at about $498,000 total, including about $294,000 build-out and $85,000 equipment. Cafe guides give $150,000-$280,000 for a small second-generation shop.","source":"https://wilsonklee.com/guides/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true},{"claim":"Build-out costs vary widely by space condition. Cafe guides cite $30-$80 per sq ft for second-generation food space and $80-$250 per sq ft for a raw shell. A second-generation space can save $40,000-$80,000. Tenant-improvement allowances for food and beverage space typically run $10-$60 per sq ft.","source":"https://tabres.com/articles/how-much-does-it-cost-to-open-a-coffee-shop","verified":true},{"claim":"College Park retail asking rents vary by site. LoopNet lists an average of about $36 per sq ft per year. Specific listings run from about $16-$25 up to $45-$50 per sq ft per year, with typical retail space of 1,000-2,500 sq ft.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/search/retail-space/college-park-md/for-lease/","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County regulates food service facilities. A new or remodeled facility needs plan review before work starts, with scaled drawings, a menu, a HACCP plan, equipment cut sheets and review fees. It then needs a Health Department Food Service Facility permit and a Use & Occupancy permit from the Department of Permitting, Inspections and Enforcement (DPIE).","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/permits/use-occupancy-permit","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a County-certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times for restaurant-type operators. Moderate and high priority facilities need one on the premises during all operating hours, plus an approved HACCP plan. A second site would therefore need its own certified manager coverage in addition to the owner's.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/departments-offices/permitting-inspections-and-enforcement/licensing/health-licenses/food-service-facility-fsf-permits","verified":true},{"claim":"Maryland's statewide minimum wage is $15.00 in 2026 and the tipped cash wage is $3.63. Prince George's County has a higher local minimum of $15.30 per hour effective January 1, 2026. Wage floors apply to any new hires at either site or for delivery.","source":"https://www.employerpass.com/maryland-minimum-wage","verified":true},{"claim":"Marketplace delivery commissions are about 15%-30% per delivery order. DoorDash tiers are 15%, 25% and 30%, and Uber Eats tiers are 20%, 25% and 30% after a March 2026 increase. Pickup orders cost about 6%-7%. Several sources say all-in effective cost, with promotions and fees, can reach 30%-40% of the order.","source":"https://www.blenderdigital.co/blog/how-much-do-doordash-uber-eats-charge-restaurants","verified":true},{"claim":"The owner's current POS (Square) offers Square Online ordering with no commission: $0 per month on Free, $49 per month on Plus, and $149 per month on Premium. Online card fees are 3.3% + $0.30 on Free and 2.9% + $0.30 on Plus and Premium. Delivery is dispatched through on-demand couriers at about $6.99 per delivery within 5 miles, and the fee can be charged to the customer.","source":"https://menuthere.com/en/alternatives/square-online/pricing","verified":true},{"claim":"One comparison estimates that absorbing the courier fee on a $40 own-channel Square delivery costs about $8.45, which is $6.99 plus card fees. Own-channel delivery pays off most clearly when the customer pays the delivery fee and on larger orders.","source":"https://paymentreview.com/blog/restaurant-online-ordering-fees/","verified":true},{"claim":"Retail bakery net profit margins are commonly cited as 4%-9%, with other sources giving 5%-15%. Labor is usually 25%-35% of revenue and food cost 28%-35%. On $42,000 per month, a 4%-9% margin would be roughly $1,700-$3,800 per month before owner pay. These benchmarks are not specific to this bakery.","source":"https://www.crestmontcapital.com/blog/bakery-business-financing-statistics-2026","verified":true},{"claim":"Bakery-cafe prime costs (COGS plus labor) are cited at 60%-70% of revenue. One source puts the single-location revenue ceiling for a dine-in bakery-cafe at $500K-$900K per year. The owner's current run rate is about $504K per year.","source":"https://advisedspaces.com/open/bakery","verified":true},{"claim":"Commercial leases for small restaurant tenants typically run 5-10 years and often include personal guarantees and NNN charges (taxes, insurance, common-area costs). A shopping-center listing in College Park shows NNN terms of 5-10 years. Personal guarantees are from general knowledge and not confirmed by search.","source":"https://www.commercialsearch.com/commercial-property/us/md/college-park/college-park-shopping-center-1/","verified":true},{"claim":"A bakery with one owner-baker and no manager faces key-person risk when expanding. Production is typically centralized or duplicated for a second site, and the owner's time is split between two sites. This is general operating knowledge, not from a search result.","source":"","verified":false}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's size, asking rent, lease type (NNN or gross), term, personal-guarantee requirement and tenant-improvement allowance?","Is the space a second-generation bakery or restaurant space with a hood, grease trap and restrooms already in place, or does it need a full build-out? What are the contractor quotes and permit timeline?","What is the first store's real monthly profit and owner take-home after paying the owner a market wage? $42,000 in revenue says nothing about margin.","How much of the $90,000 is already needed for working capital or the six-month runway? What would remain after build-out, equipment, deposits and pre-opening losses, and could SBA or other financing supplement it?","Who would manage the first store, or the second, while the owner bakes each morning? What would a hired manager cost, and could the manager hold the Prince George's County Food Service Manager certification?","Can the current kitchen handle added delivery volume, or a second store's production, without new equipment or labor?","How much demand exists for delivery among current customers and nearby residents and students? How seasonal is revenue with the University of Maryland calendar, and would a Route 1 site face the same seasonality or cannibalize the first store?","Do the existing Instagram and Google Business Profile traffic and the current order mix (coffee, pastries, custom orders) point toward delivery-friendly products?","Are there any current Prince George's County or College Park rules on third-party delivery fees, signage or zoning for this Route 1 parcel? The search did not confirm any."]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/researcher@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/researcher@1"]},"id":"e6aee788-5060-4162-8ede-64ebe1861978","timestamp":1794033241.698429}

data: {"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 1 — give your independent opinion. You have not seen the other seats yet.","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/convene@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/convene@1"]},"id":"a5aee03c-31ea-4382-a531-96ee2b47d725","timestamp":1794033274.1294694}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't open Route 1 now; build delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"A second store needs far more than $90,000 (F1), and your first store has no manager while you bake every morning. Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand. Build cash, train a manager, and revisit the lease after 6-12 months of data.\",\"key_points\":[\"A second-generation bakery runs about $498,000 all-in (F1), more than five times your savings, before any runway is counted.\",\"Your run rate is near the single-store ceiling (F11), and a typical 4%-9% margin means roughly $1,700-$3,800 a month (F10) that a second site could easily erase.\",\"Own-channel Square delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions (F7, F8), so it is a low-risk way to test demand first.\"],\"risks\":[\"Lease commitment of 5-10 years with personal guarantees and NNN charges could put your home and first store at risk (F12).\",\"Without a manager, you would split your morning baking time across two sites and need a second certified food service manager (F5, F13).\",\"Delivery fees can eat margin on small orders; absorbing a $6.99 courier fee on a $40 order costs about $8.45 (F9).\"],\"confidence\":78,\"pushback\":\"Assume the Route 1 space is a good deal. Get its size, rent, build-out status and lease terms before deciding; a turnkey second-generation space with a landlord allowance (F2) would change this analysis.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":494,"promptTokenCount":3967,"totalTokenCount":4461},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"industry_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't open Route 1 now; build delivery and a manager first.","summary":"A second store needs far more than $90,000 (F1), and your first store has no manager while you bake every morning. Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand. Build cash, train a manager, and revisit the lease after 6-12 months of data.","key_points":["A second-generation bakery runs about $498,000 all-in (F1), more than five times your savings, before any runway is counted.","Your run rate is near the single-store ceiling (F11), and a typical 4%-9% margin means roughly $1,700-$3,800 a month (F10) that a second site could easily erase.","Own-channel Square delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions (F7, F8), so it is a low-risk way to test demand first."],"risks":["Lease commitment of 5-10 years with personal guarantees and NNN charges could put your home and first store at risk (F12).","Without a manager, you would split your morning baking time across two sites and need a second certified food service manager (F5, F13).","Delivery fees can eat margin on small orders; absorbing a $6.99 courier fee on a $40 order costs about $8.45 (F9)."],"confidence":78,"pushback":"Assume the Route 1 space is a good deal. Get its size, rent, build-out status and lease terms before deciding; a turnkey second-generation space with a landlord allowance (F2) would change this analysis."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r1@1","id":"9ec45d70-ced7-4c6c-b5e1-08be7ecd5b8e","timestamp":1794033274.149849}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't open Route 1 yet; build delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"A second store likely costs far more than your $90,000 and would drain the runway that protects your first store. Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand cheaply. Hire or train a manager before any expansion. Revisit Route 1 after 6-12 months of real data.\",\"key_points\":[\"A second-generation bakery runs about $150,000-$500,000 total, well above your savings (F1, F2).\",\"Your owner-baker role has no backup; a second site splits your time and needs its own certified manager coverage (F5, F13).\",\"Own-channel Square delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions and costs little to start (F7, F8).\"],\"risks\":[\"Delay could let a good Route 1 lease go to someone else; that cost is real but recoverable.\",\"Delivery may draw little demand, and a few months of tests would show that cheaply.\",\"Hiring a manager adds payroll at $15.30/hour minimum before revenue proves it (F6).\"],\"confidence\":75,\"pushback\":\"You assume the Route 1 space is a good deal. Get its size, rent, lease terms, and build-out quote first. If it is already a built food-service space with a cheap lease, the case changes.(unverified)\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":434,"promptTokenCount":3963,"totalTokenCount":4397},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"strategist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't open Route 1 yet; build delivery and a manager first.","summary":"A second store likely costs far more than your $90,000 and would drain the runway that protects your first store. Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand cheaply. Hire or train a manager before any expansion. Revisit Route 1 after 6-12 months of real data.","key_points":["A second-generation bakery runs about $150,000-$500,000 total, well above your savings (F1, F2).","Your owner-baker role has no backup; a second site splits your time and needs its own certified manager coverage (F5, F13).","Own-channel Square delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions and costs little to start (F7, F8)."],"risks":["Delay could let a good Route 1 lease go to someone else; that cost is real but recoverable.","Delivery may draw little demand, and a few months of tests would show that cheaply.","Hiring a manager adds payroll at $15.30/hour minimum before revenue proves it (F6)."],"confidence":75,"pushback":"You assume the Route 1 space is a good deal. Get its size, rent, lease terms, and build-out quote first. If it is already a built food-service space with a cheap lease, the case changes.(unverified)"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r1@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r1@1","id":"61a425c9-0f9c-4eb3-a4be-3a773fe4d3d8","timestamp":1794033274.1535912}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't open Route 1 yet; test delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"Opening a second store with $90,000 risks your first store, which pays for everything. Build-out alone can run far past your savings (F1). You also bake every morning and have no manager, so a second site splits you thin (F13). Delivery through your own Square channel costs little to test first and shows real demand before you sign a long lease.\",\"key_points\":[\"A $90,000 budget is far below typical second-store costs of $150,000-$500,000 (F1), and a 5-10 year lease with personal guarantees could follow you (F12).\",\"Your margin is unknown; a typical 4%-9% bakery margin on $42,000 a month is about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10). Six months of runway is thin.\",\"County rules require a certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times at each site (F5), and you have no manager yet.\"],\"risks\":[\"Route 1 build-out or permit delays drain savings before the store earns a dollar, and the first store has no cushion left.\",\"Your own time gets split, quality slips at the original store, and revenue drops there while the new one is still ramping.\",\"Lease terms (NNN charges, personal guarantee) lock you into costs for years even if the second store underperforms.\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the Route 1 opportunity is one-time. Ask whether a smaller or second-generation space can be found in 6-12 months, and whether delivery demand exists before you commit to a second kitchen.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":507,"promptTokenCount":4016,"totalTokenCount":4523},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"devils_advocate_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't open Route 1 yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Opening a second store with $90,000 risks your first store, which pays for everything. Build-out alone can run far past your savings (F1). You also bake every morning and have no manager, so a second site splits you thin (F13). Delivery through your own Square channel costs little to test first and shows real demand before you sign a long lease.","key_points":["A $90,000 budget is far below typical second-store costs of $150,000-$500,000 (F1), and a 5-10 year lease with personal guarantees could follow you (F12).","Your margin is unknown; a typical 4%-9% bakery margin on $42,000 a month is about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10). Six months of runway is thin.","County rules require a certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times at each site (F5), and you have no manager yet."],"risks":["Route 1 build-out or permit delays drain savings before the store earns a dollar, and the first store has no cushion left.","Your own time gets split, quality slips at the original store, and revenue drops there while the new one is still ramping.","Lease terms (NNN charges, personal guarantee) lock you into costs for years even if the second store underperforms."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes the Route 1 opportunity is one-time. Ask whether a smaller or second-generation space can be found in 6-12 months, and whether delivery demand exists before you commit to a second kitchen."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r1@1","id":"684c1355-7845-460a-ae52-ec374b532161","timestamp":1794033274.1554313}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Don't open Route 1 now; start delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"A second store would likely cost far more than your $90,000 (F1), and you would need a certified food manager on site at both places (F5). Your six months of savings (F-brief) is your safety net, and a new lease could use it up fast. Start low-cost pickup and delivery ordering now, hire or train a manager, and revisit a second site after 6-12 months of real numbers.\",\"key_points\":[\"A second-generation bakery build may total about $498,000 (F1); $90,000 is not enough without large financing.\",\"Prince George's County requires a certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times, so a second site needs its own coverage (F5).\",\"Own-channel Square Online delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions (F7, F8), with the $6.99 courier fee often passed to the customer (F9).\"],\"risks\":[\"Lease commitments of 5-10 years with possible personal guarantees and NNN charges could outlast the savings (F12).\",\"Your own time is split between two sites while you still bake every morning (F13, unverified).\",\"Delivery margins are thin; at a 4%-9% net margin, $42,000 a month yields about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10).\"],\"confidence\":75,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the Route 1 space would be a second-generation, low-cost build; the lease terms, space condition and contractor quotes are still unknown, so the true cost could be lower or much higher.\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"A second location may need roughly $150,000-$500,000 (F1), far above the $90,000 savings, so it cannot be funded from savings alone without financing (unverified: SBA options via the Small Business Development Center).\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"A second site needs plan review, a Health Department permit and a Use & Occupancy permit (F4), plus its own certified manager coverage (F5); confirm with the Prince George's County Health Department.\"},{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Route 1 lease terms, personal guarantees and NNN charges are unknown (F12); have an attorney review before signing anything.\"},{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Current profit is unknown; a 4%-9% margin on $42,000 monthly revenue gives about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10), which is thin cushion for a new lease.\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"No manager and an owner who bakes each morning is a key-person risk for two sites (F13, unverified); hiring at $15.30 an hour minimum in PG County applies (F6).\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"Own-channel delivery supports revenue growth with low fixed cost and keeps the first store protected (F7, F8, F9).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":956,"promptTokenCount":4353,"totalTokenCount":5309},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"realist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Don't open Route 1 now; start delivery and a manager first.","summary":"A second store would likely cost far more than your $90,000 (F1), and you would need a certified food manager on site at both places (F5). Your six months of savings (F-brief) is your safety net, and a new lease could use it up fast. Start low-cost pickup and delivery ordering now, hire or train a manager, and revisit a second site after 6-12 months of real numbers.","key_points":["A second-generation bakery build may total about $498,000 (F1); $90,000 is not enough without large financing.","Prince George's County requires a certified Food Service Manager on duty at all times, so a second site needs its own coverage (F5).","Own-channel Square Online delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions (F7, F8), with the $6.99 courier fee often passed to the customer (F9)."],"risks":["Lease commitments of 5-10 years with possible personal guarantees and NNN charges could outlast the savings (F12).","Your own time is split between two sites while you still bake every morning (F13, unverified).","Delivery margins are thin; at a 4%-9% net margin, $42,000 a month yields about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10)."],"confidence":75,"pushback":"Assumes the Route 1 space would be a second-generation, low-cost build; the lease terms, space condition and contractor quotes are still unknown, so the true cost could be lower or much higher.","checks":[{"area":"financial","status":"block","note":"A second location may need roughly $150,000-$500,000 (F1), far above the $90,000 savings, so it cannot be funded from savings alone without financing (unverified: SBA options via the Small Business Development Center)."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"A second site needs plan review, a Health Department permit and a Use & Occupancy permit (F4), plus its own certified manager coverage (F5); confirm with the Prince George's County Health Department."},{"area":"legal","status":"flag","note":"Route 1 lease terms, personal guarantees and NNN charges are unknown (F12); have an attorney review before signing anything."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"Current profit is unknown; a 4%-9% margin on $42,000 monthly revenue gives about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10), which is thin cushion for a new lease."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"No manager and an owner who bakes each morning is a key-person risk for two sites (F13, unverified); hiring at $15.30 an hour minimum in PG County applies (F6)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Own-channel delivery supports revenue growth with low fixed cost and keeps the first store protected (F7, F8, F9)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r1@1"]},"branch":"realist_r1@1","id":"cf2e0686-b0ad-4dc8-8a88-80afcd15608b","timestamp":1794033274.1521292}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't open Route 1 now; fix margins and add delivery first.\",\"summary\":\"A second location would likely need far more than your $90,000, and it would strain your only store while you bake every morning. Your first store earns an estimated $1,700-$3,800 a month before owner pay (unverified benchmark). Build a manager and a profit record first. Add commission-free Square Online delivery now, then reconsider a smaller or second-generation space in 6-12 months.\",\"key_points\":[\"A 1,400 sq ft second-generation bakery can cost about $498,000 all-in (F1), and cafe guides run $150,000-$280,000 (F1). Your $90,000 covers only part of that.\",\"Prince George's County requires a certified Food Service Manager on duty during all operating hours at each site (F5). You have no manager yet (F13).\",\"Square Online charges no commission (F8), while marketplace apps take 15%-30% per order (F7). Start there before paying third-party fees.\"],\"risks\":[\"Losing the first store's income or quality while you split time across two sites and lean on savings for six months.\",\"Long lease terms of 5-10 years, often with personal guarantees and NNN charges (F12), could outlast the business.\",\"Build-out and permit delays (F4) would add cost before the second store earns anything.\"],\"confidence\":82,\"pushback\":\"Assume the Route 1 space is a bargain only if its build-out and rent are verified; ask for contractor quotes and the lease terms before deciding.\",\"tools\":[]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1033,"promptTokenCount":4486,"thoughtsTokenCount":535,"totalTokenCount":5519},"invocationId":"e-76de84ea-b4c0-4e28-b8d7-1ad19b78cfab","author":"ai_tools_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't open Route 1 now; fix margins and add delivery first.","summary":"A second location would likely need far more than your $90,000, and it would strain your only store while you bake every morning. Your first store earns an estimated $1,700-$3,800 a month before owner pay (unverified benchmark). Build a manager and a profit record first. Add commission-free Square Online delivery now, then reconsider a smaller or second-generation space in 6-12 months.","key_points":["A 1,400 sq ft second-generation bakery can cost about $498,000 all-in (F1), and cafe guides run $150,000-$280,000 (F1). Your $90,000 covers only part of that.","Prince George's County requires a certified Food Service Manager on duty during all operating hours at each site (F5). You have no manager yet (F13).","Square Online charges no commission (F8), while marketplace apps take 15%-30% per order (F7). Start there before paying third-party fees."],"risks":["Losing the first store's income or quality while you split time across two sites and lean on savings for six months.","Long lease terms of 5-10 years, often with personal guarantees and NNN charges (F12), could outlast the business.","Build-out and permit delays (F4) would add cost before the second store earns anything."],"confidence":82,"pushback":"Assume the Route 1 space is a bargain only if its build-out and rent are verified; ask for contractor quotes and the lease terms before deciding.","tools":[]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r1@1","id":"a1ab3ad3-6afa-4d44-b9a4-f33f2facafde","timestamp":1794033274.1510355}

