 needs plan review, a Health Department permit and a Use & Occupancy permit (F4), plus its own certified manager coverage (F5); confirm with the Prince George's County Health Department."},{"area":"legal","status":"flag","note":"Route 1 lease terms, personal guarantees and NNN charges are unknown (F12); have an attorney review before signing anything."},{"area":"financial","status":"flag","note":"Current profit is unknown; a 4%-9% margin on $42,000 monthly revenue gives about $1,700-$3,800 before owner pay (F10), which is thin cushion for a new lease."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"No manager and an owner who bakes each morning is a key-person risk for two sites (F13, unverified); hiring at $15.30 an hour minimum in PG County applies (F6)."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Own-channel delivery supports revenue growth with low fixed cost and keeps the first store protected (F7, F8, F9)."}]},"industry_expert_r1":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't open Route 1 now; build delivery and a manager first.","summary":"A second store needs far more than $90,000 (F1), and your first store has no manager while you bake every morning. Delivery through your own Square channel costs little and tests demand. Build cash, train a manager, and revisit the lease after 6-12 months of data.","key_points":["A second-generation bakery runs about $498,000 all-in (F1), more than five times your savings, before any runway is counted.","Your run rate is near the single-store ceiling (F11), and a typical 4%-9% margin means roughly $1,700-$3,800 a month (F10) that a second site could easily erase.","Own-channel Square delivery avoids 15%-30% marketplace commissions (F7, F8), so it is a low-risk way to test demand first."],"risks":["Lease commitment of 5-10 years with personal guarantees and NNN charges could put your home and first store at risk (F12).","Without a manager, you would split your morning baking time across two sites and need a second certified food servic